const body = `<h1>Method Signatures</h1>

<p>
    A signature is the one line that defines a method. Everything else, the body,
    the algorithms, the cleverness, is hidden behind it. Reading code fast means
    reading signatures fast, and the AP exam is mostly signatures: the JQR is
    signatures, FRQ specs are signatures, method headers you must complete are
    signatures.
</p>

<h2>Anatomy</h2>

<pre><code>public static int roll(int sides)
│      │      │   │    │
│      │      │   │    └─ parameter list: type name, type name...
│      │      │   └─ method name (lowerCamelCase)
│      │      └─ return type: int, double, boolean, String, void, or a class name
│      └─ static = belongs to the class; omit = instance method (needs an object)
└─ visibility: public (the API), private (the internals)</code></pre>

<h2>Decode These Six</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Signature</th><th>In plain English</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>public static int abs(int x)</code></td>
        <td>Class method. Give it an int, get an int back. No object needed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>public int length()</code></td>
        <td>Instance method. Call it on a String. Returns an int. No inputs.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>public String substring(int from, int to)</code></td>
        <td>Instance method. Two ints in, a new String out.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>public boolean add(E obj)</code></td>
        <td>Instance method (ArrayList). Any object in, true/false out.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>public static void main(String[] args)</code></td>
        <td>Class method. Returns nothing. Takes an array of Strings (we ignore it).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>public E remove(int index)</code></td>
        <td>Instance method. Index in, the removed element OUT. It returns the thing
        it removed. Programs have been written on this fact alone.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Contract Violations: Which Compile?</h2>

<p>Given <code>public static int roll(int sides)</code>:</p>

<table>
    <tr><th>Call</th><th>Compiles?</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>roll(20)</code></td><td>yes</td><td>int into int parameter</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>roll("20")</code></td><td>NO</td><td>String is not int. No automatic conversion for parameters.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>roll()</code></td><td>NO</td><td>missing parameter, exactly one required</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>roll(20, 6)</code></td><td>NO</td><td>too many parameters</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>int r = roll(20);</code></td><td>yes</td><td>int return into int variable</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>double d = roll(20);</code></td><td>yes</td><td>int widens to double automatically</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>roll(20);</code></td><td>yes</td><td>return value discarded. Legal. Often a bug.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>String s = roll(20);</code></td><td>NO</td><td>int does not become String</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Overloading: Same Name, Different Contract</h2>

<p>Two methods can share a name if their parameter lists differ. Java picks by
matching your argument types:</p>

<pre><code>public static int attack(String target) { ... }
public static int attack(int damage)   { ... }

attack("goblin");   // calls the String version
attack(15);         // calls the int version</code></pre>

<p>You have used overloading since Unit 1: <code>Math.abs</code> exists as both
int and double versions. <code>substring(int)</code> and
<code>substring(int, int)</code> are overloads. Constructors with different
parameter lists (Unit 5) are overloading too. Return type alone does NOT
distinguish overloads; the parameter list must differ.</p>

<h2>Writing a Signature From English</h2>

<p>The exam asks for both directions. English to signature:</p>

<div class="callout lab">
    <p>"A static method that takes a String name and returns true if the name is a
    boss monster, false otherwise."</p>
    <p>Steps: static, so <code>public static</code>. Returns true/false, so
    <code>boolean</code>. Name it honestly: <code>isBoss</code>. One parameter, a
    String:</p>
    <pre><code>public static boolean isBoss(String name)</code></pre>
    <p>"A method on a Potion object that returns its price as a double, no
    parameters."</p>
    <pre><code>public double getPrice()</code></pre>
    <p>Instance method (it needs a particular potion's data), no static, no
    parameters, double return.</p>
</div>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: Signature Detective</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>From the JQR Alone</h3>
    <p>Open the <a href="../../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick
    Reference</a>. For each of these, decode the signature and then verify with a
    two-line program:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><code>static int parseInt(String s)</code> — what goes in? what comes
        out? what is notably ABSENT from the signature that tells you it does not
        need an object?</li>
        <li><code>E set(int index, E obj)</code> — it returns the element formerly
        at that position. Write a program where that return value is the whole
        point (swap two list elements using two set calls and one temp).</li>
        <li><code>String[] split(String del)</code> — what kind of thing comes
        back? What does its length tell you?</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why does the return type alone not distinguish overloads?</h3>
    <p>Because Java chooses the overload at the call site, using the arguments.
    <code>attack(15)</code> does not know what you intend to do with the return
    value; the arguments alone must select the method. If two overloads differed
    only by return type, the compiler could not pick. So the language forbids it.</p>
    <h3>Why is discarding a return value legal?</h3>
    <p>Because sometimes you only want the side effect: <code>list.add(x);</code>
    returns true, always, and you almost never care. Java leaves the decision to
    you. The bug risk is real, though: <code>scanner.nextLine();</code> looks like
    it does nothing when it actually ate a line. Discard deliberately or not at
    all.</p>
</div>

